Upstream NOTICE attributions (Apache License 2.0 section 4(d))
================================================================================

Section 4(d) requires that we reproduce the contents of any NOTICE file
shipped by an Apache-2.0 licensed work we redistribute.

cls-studio is distributed as source only, so it redistributes no
third-party code at all: dependencies are installed from PyPI and npm
onto the user's own machine. The Apache-2.0 licensed components that a
default install pulls in there are:

  python-multipart, fasteners, opencv-python-headless

Optional --with-openvino installs additionally pull these Apache-2.0
packages from PyPI onto the user's machine:

  openvino, nncf, openvino-telemetry, safetensors, ninja
  (ninja is Apache-2.0 AND BSD-3-Clause; packaging, also pulled in, is
   dual Apache-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause and is taken under BSD-2-Clause)

None of these projects publishes a NOTICE file upstream (re-verified
2026-07-29 against each project's repository root — every name in both
lists above). There is therefore nothing to reproduce here. If any
upstream adds a NOTICE file in future, it will be incorporated at the
next dependency refresh.

For the complete list of third-party dependencies and their licenses, see
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.

================================================================================
Copyleft pulled in at install time (not redistributed here)
================================================================================

opencv-python-headless bundles its own FFmpeg build inside the wheel that
pip installs on the user's machine: an LGPL-2.1+ build in the Windows
wheel, and a GPL build (--enable-gpl, x264/x265) in the macOS wheel.
cls-studio redistributes neither -- pip fetches the wheel from PyPI
directly -- so no copyleft obligation attaches to this project.

  FFmpeg (via cv2)  LGPL-2.1+ (Windows wheel) / GPL (macOS wheel)
                    https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg

Anyone repackaging cls-studio into a binary distribution inherits those
obligations. The macOS wheel is the sharp edge: cv2.abi3.so links libav*
through @loader_path, so the GPL build cannot be stripped out.

================================================================================
NVIDIA libraries via PyTorch CUDA wheel (CUDA installs)
================================================================================

A CUDA install pip-installs the PyTorch CUDA wheel onto the user's own
machine. That wheel transitively bundles the NVIDIA libraries pinned as
nvidia-*-cu12 in apps/api/requirements.txt, under their respective
redistributable licenses. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md enumerates that pin set; the lockfile
itself is authoritative, and cuSPARSELt in particular carries its own
NVIDIA license terms. cls-studio does not download or package these
libraries from NVIDIA directly, and does not redistribute them; they
arrive from the PyTorch wheel that PyTorch's maintainers publish under the
permissions granted by NVIDIA.

On Linux these arrive as separate nvidia-*-cu12 wheels; on Windows the
equivalent DLLs are bundled inside the torch wheel itself (torch/lib/).

